            <div xml:id="d816">

                <pb facs="1010" n="886" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg886"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">422.11 G 93/1174</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hartman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-04-04"><hi rendition="#italic">April 4,
                        1921</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received May 6.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 668</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to confirm my telegram No.
                    11 of April 3, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="18" xml:id="d816-f1"/> wherein I reported the
                    sincere desire of the Government of Ecuador to remit the monies now on deposit,
                    amounting to more than one million sucres, for the service of the Guayaquil
                    &amp; Quito Railway bonds, and suggested that if the Department could induce the
                    bondholders to accept cacao in payment of all or part of the amount now on
                    deposit, it would be greatly appreciated by the Government of Ecuador.</p>
                <p>I am thoroughly convinced of the good faith of the President and Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs in their expressed desire to devise some plan to enable them to
                    remit this money to London as soon as possible, but they feel that the present
                    rate of exchange is ruinous, and they are therefore invoking the aid of the
                    Department to assist in inducing the bondholders to accept cacao in lieu of
                    money. Within a few days I expect to have another interview with President
                    Tamayo on the general financial situation, particularly with reference to the
                    present status of the negotiations for the conversion of the foreign debt. After
                    that interview I will report results to the Department.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chas. S. Hartman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
